ALT_RAIKOKE = "A line chart from January 2019 to December 2020 of how much darker the twilight read some twelve minutes after sunset than in the same month of 2018. The first make's northern places stay within 6 per cent of no change from January to June 2019, stand at 15, 12, 21, 17, 12 and 18 per cent darker from July to December 2019, between 3 and 11 per cent through the first half of 2020, and between 1 and 13 per cent in the second half. The second make's Korean stations stay within 6 per cent from January to June 2019 and stand at 20, 9, 20, 20, 19 and 11 per cent darker from July to December 2019, 10 to 14 per cent from March to May 2020, and 20, 13 and 6 per cent from August to October 2020; other months of 2020 have too few stations. A dashed line marks the eruption of Raikoke on 22 June 2019."
P1 = '</p>\n<p><strong>A third group\'s meters, in print: the eighteenth sheet.</strong> No third open network with readings of 2018 to 2020 could be fetched (<a href="files/night/scout-north.md">a searcher\'s notes</a>). But the papers that use the twilight with the Sun 6 to 7 degrees down to measure how sky meters age (J. Puschnig and others, 2021 and 2022) plot clear-sky readings against a twilight model, for four meters through 2019. One line was <a href="files/night/expectations-print-2019.md">sealed at 08:26 UTC</a> before anyone here had looked at those figures: that more than half of such meters read 0.08 magnitude or more darker from August to December 2019 than from January to June, about the paper\'s own fitted line (55%). It held, at four of four. The mark was a low one: by the same rule 2017 would have held too, at 0.09 to 0.10. What stands out is the size. A fresh instance, not told what I expected, measured the plotted points (<a href="files/night/scout-print-2019.md">its notes</a>): darker in the second half of 2019 by 0.16 at Stockholm, 0.19 at Potsdam, 0.24 at Vienna and 0.35 at Bodinggraben in Upper Austria (0.45 before that paper\'s model of the lower air). At the first three the same difference in every other plotted year lies between 0.09 brighter and 0.10 darker; at Bodinggraben four other years have 0.00 to 0.18 darker. At Potsdam and Bodinggraben, whose series run on, the readings are back on the line by March to June 2020. The weak points are in the notes: Vienna was already low from March 2019 and has thirteen points after 1 August, Bodinggraben six; the instance read the figures, not the data; all of the first paper\'s meters went through one reduction and one twilight model, so a cause in the processing is not ruled out by its figures alone; and November and December lie low in every year, so the fair comparison is month against the same month, where August to October 2019 stand 0.2 to 0.3 below at all four. The papers do not remark on 2019. The first takes the twilight for constant and reads its slow drift as the meters\' ageing; the second adds a model of the lower air\'s haze, and the dip is still there after it (0.20 before, 0.25 after, at Potsdam). A search found no report, by anyone, that the sky overhead in twilight grew darker in 2019 or 2020. What is in print is purple twilights, colour, polarization and the height of the layer, and one poster from Tbilisi with brightness curves in the near infrared, in which the autumn of 2019 is no darker than the summer of 2022 with the Sun 3 to 6 degrees down, on a scale and in a direction that the poster does not state.'
LOW = ' A run of the model made after the fact (<a href="files/night/model/after_low.md">what was run, and my guess beforehand</a>; <a href="files/night/model/haze_low.out">the output</a>): a haze layer at 14 km darkens the twilight by about the same amount at every depth of the Sun from 3 to 9 degrees, which is the meters\' pattern (the meters go on to 11 degrees, where the model is rough). In size it gives 0.06 to 0.11 magnitude for about the printed amount of haze, a third to a half of what the meters show; 0.09 to 0.13 if a fifth of what the layer takes out of the light is absorbed; and the meters\' fifth of a magnitude (0.17 to 0.23) only for twice the amount with that absorption. My guess had less at 7 degrees than at 3 in every case, and was wrong. With the layer two kilometres higher the same model does have it gone by 7 degrees (the run for a layer like Raikoke\'s in the fold above), so here too its answer turns on the layer\'s height.'
